  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Potential for strong storms with heavy downpours \ntoday across mainly the north. \n\nA cold front is currently analyzed over southern VA, with cool NE \nflow and low stratus to its north. Ahead of the front, heating and \ndestabilization have occurred, with widespread temperatures in the \nupper-80s to lower-90s across central NC and MLCAPE in the 1000-2000 \nJ/kg range, highest over the NW Piedmont. Scattered cumulus has \nbegun to develop, with a few isolated showers already popping up \nover parts of the northern and western Piedmont. The deeper \nconvection is located along a convergence axis over western VA/NC as \na shortwave approaches from western KY. \n\nHigh-res models are in good agreement that convection will continue \nto expand this afternoon, though how far SE it makes before \nnocturnal stabilization is still uncertain as latest runs of the \nHRRR and NAMNest keep the heaviest rain just north of our CWA. Where \nstorms do occur, locally excessive rainfall will be possible, with \nthe greatest threat near the NC/VA border. 12z HREF LPMM indicates \npotential for isolated 1-3\"+ amounts in those areas, which makes \nsense given above-normal PW values of 1.6-2 inches and very weak \nflow and shear (0-6km mean winds only around 5 kts) resulting in \nvery slow-moving storms and possible merging and training of cells. \nGiven dry antecedent conditions, think any flash flooding would be \nisolated and mainly limited to urban areas.\n\nAs for the severe threat, SPC has areas from RDU north and west in a \nmarginal (level 1 of 5) risk today. The main threat is damaging \nwinds given steep low-level lapse rates and DCAPE as high as 900 \nJ/kg over the NW Piedmont. Any severe threat should diminish after \ndark.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Additional localized heavy rain possible areawide \non Tuesday.  \n\nCentral NC will likely be positioned between the leading edge of the \nsynoptic cold front in VA, later to be established as the CAD front, \nand convective outflow and/or diff heating boundary from Mon/Mon \nnight convection. A moderately unstable air mass with weak shear, \ntall skinny CAPE, above normal PWAT, and an approaching shortwave \ntrough from the Ohio Valley into the Southern Appalachians will all \nfavor an increase in convective coverage with slow moving storms, \nthat may persist into the overnight period, capable of torrential \nrainfall and increased flooding risk (level 2 out of 4 from WPC \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Cold air damming will bring much below normal \ntemperatures during mid to late week. \n\nAs the surface front settles S of NC, confidence is high in the \ndevelopment of a robust, classical cold air damming event this week. \nWith an early-week rex block in place over the Upper Midwest and \nManitoba/Ontario, mid level energy will undercut the blocking high, \ncrossing IN/OH before deepening to a low over the Mid Atlantic \nregion during the mid-late week. This will keep the surface frontal \nzone pinned to our S and SE, with steady advection of cool and low-\ndewpoint surface air from a fairly strong and anchored surface high \ncentered over Quebec. This high will weaken as it slowly retrogrades \nto the WSW late Thu into Fri, drifting into the N Great Lakes and S \nOntario in response to what is expected to be strong coastal \ncyclogenesis just off the Mid Atlantic and Northeast coast by \nFri/Sat, but the circulation around such a low alone should maintain \nthe wedge regime and low level CAA through at least Fri, despite the \nlikelihood of further drying aloft potentially eroding much of the \nCAD stratus by late in the day.\n\nThe result will be multiple days of high temps 10-15 deg F below \nnormal, including highs from the Triangle to the N and W in the 60s \nto around 70 each day Wed through Fri. These temps will be further \nheld down by copious cloud cover and widespread overrunning rain, \ndriven in large part by moist isentropic upglide focused at 295K-\n305K from Wed morning through Thu night. In addition to the cool \ndays, we're likely to see a few nighttime lows in the 40s near the \nVA border Thu night through Sat night. Rain chances are expected to \ndwindle Fri and remain low into the weekend, as low-level stability \ndecreases with the weakening and retrograding surface ridge. But the \npotential for a baggy mid level trough or shear axis holding across \nthe region along with a persistent low level flow from the N or NNW \naround the coastal low should result in highs remaining at least a \ncategory below normal Sat/Sun. Given that we've seen above to much \nabove-normal temps for all but a couple of days so far this month, \nseveral days of below to much-below normal temps will be a welcome \nrespite for many.",
